Subject: chimera: a fade of a cutscene takes the frames the machine needs

A cutscene frame draws random numbers (two torches, and a star on odd
frame counts), so how many frames a fade takes decides where the random
stream stands when the next level starts. SDLPoP times a fade step at 2
timer ticks, three to a 6-tick cutscene frame: the 64 steps of a fade take
22 frames. The DOS game does not time them: a step lasts as long as it
takes and a cutscene frame lets steps through until its own timer has run
out. So the number is the machine's. Measured on Prince of Persia 1.4 under
DOSBox-X (the cutscene before level 4, and at the default speed those
before levels 2, 6, 8, 9 and 12 too):

    CPU cycles   22000 (default), 6000   2300, 1800   1400   1000   700   600 and less
    frames       18                      22           25     33     51    64 in, 65 out

At 600 cycles and below a frame gets through one step only, the most a
fade can take.

Under CHIMERA_CORE a cutscene frame of a fade lets through the steps that
bring the fade to frame * 64 / chimera_fade_frames(), whatever the timer
says. The core answers with its fade_frames setting (18 by default). With
18 and with 64 the frames and the draws of a cutscene are the DOS game's,
step for step; the speeds in between are not regular on DOS (a fade out
takes one to four frames more than a fade in) and are only approximated.

